So out of this worry, we have something called the Bitcoin, Bitcoin.

18
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:00,120
The story of Maple Protocol is a comeback story that in many ways mirrors the journey

19
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,600
of DeFi capital markets itself.

20
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:08,040
Maple founder Sid Powell left his career in traditional debt markets in 2019 to build

21
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,840
a more efficient crypto lending platform.

22
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:16,560
At the time, BlockFi and Celsius had emerged as early crypto lenders using off-chain underwriting,

23
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:20,840
while Maker and Compound had surfaced as nascent DeFi market entrants.

24
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:25,920
Powell had seen the inefficiencies of the traditional banking system up close and became

25
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:31,920
interested in the concept of capital formation outside of these traditional guardrails.

26
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:35,640
Maybe stable coins and smart contracts could remove some of the friction in underwriting

27
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:40,960
and lending and institutional grade credit could exist on chain with faster moving capital

28
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:44,560
formation and a far wider set of market participants.

29
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:50,280
Maple launched in 2021 with that vision in mind, to offer high quality DeFi credit opportunities

30
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:53,160
in a way that felt credible and compliant.

31
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:58,880
For a while, that bet was paying off as Maple gained momentum and delegates, delegates being

32
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,760
expert credit underwriters on Maple's platform and were consistently underwriting loans to

33
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:05,960
trading firms and market makers.

34
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:09,840
Maple was facilitating hundreds of millions in originations and for those watching from

35
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:13,960
the outside, it felt like the protocol had reached escape velocity.

36
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:17,280
Unfortunately, 2022 proved a difficult year.

37
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:23,400
The crypto credit contagion unfolded at scale from Celsius and BlockFi to the FTX fallout

38
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,080
and Maple found itself caught in this storm.

39
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:30,560
Major borrowers defaulted, including orthogonal trading and the full structure of the protocol's

40
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,320
first iteration was tested all at once.

41
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:38,760
Pool delegates were caught off guard, lenders suffered losses and TVL suffered.

42
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:43,920
For Powell, the founder of a now distressed protocol with a live token and real users,

43
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:46,880
the path forward was difficult and opaque.

44
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,800
On great adversity, Powell and the Maple team got to work forging a path forward.

45
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:56,160
That process started with a retrospective of what went wrong, what was salvageable and

46
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:00,000
what risk parameters would be sustainable over the long term future.

47
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,320
The team quickly identified areas for potential change.

48
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:06,960
They tightened the underwriting process and implemented stricter requirements for pool

49
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:08,360
delegates.

50
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:13,000
They also launched a new product called Syrup, a permissionless lending primitive that allows

51
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,600
users to earn yield on stablecoins without relying on a human underwriter, a delegate.

52
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:23,720
Instead of curated delegates, Syrup uses standard risk parameters and smart contract logic to

53
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:28,120
manage loans, making it significantly more scalable and accessible for a wider set of

54
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,960
lenders and borrowers.

55
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:34,120
This was a meaningful shift, a move away from centralized underwriting and towards more

56
00:03:34,120 --> 00:03:37,260
scalable rules-based infrastructure.

57
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:42,180
This wasn't an overnight turnaround and trust had to be earned back gradually, keeping in

58
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:46,820
mind that most other lenders from 2022 had completely gone away.

59
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:51,740
Maple initially re-entered the market quietly, with a focus on reliable, stablecoin-native

60
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:54,520
credit and institutional-grade partners.

61
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:58,980
Over time, TVL began to return and new borrowers came online.

62
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:03,720
Perhaps most importantly, the protocol separated itself from the CFI collapse that had defined

63
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:05,380
the previous cycle.

64
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:10,700
In typical slowly-than-all-at-once fashion, Maple made its way to $500 million in deposits

65
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:17,100
by late 2024, no small feat, and then found true escape velocity in the spring of 2025.

66
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:21,180
Today, Maple has over $3 billion in deposits.

67
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:27,700
The platform offering now looks very different from its first iteration, more streamlined,

68
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:29,980
risk-aware, and focused.

69
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:33,980
As DeFi credit markets show sign of maturity in the backdrop of the ongoing stablecoin

70
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:38,680
boom, Maple is once again at the forefront of defining what institutional capital market

71
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:40,960
formation could look like.

72
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:45,880
In this conversation, Sid and I discuss on-chain credit broadly and Maple's path to becoming

73
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:49,400
a market leader in stablecoin-powered debt markets.

74
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:51,440
I hope you enjoy our conversation.

75
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,800
Sid, thank you for joining us today.

76
00:04:54,800 --> 00:05:00,160
I wanted to say from the outset, one thing I admire about you and the team at Maple is

77
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:05,280
for as long as I've known you in the protocol, it feels like while there have been inflection

78
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:09,440
points in growth and there have been times when things have been slower, I don't see

79
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:12,520
you guys as having changed attitude in any way.

80
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:16,320
It seems like you've been fairly consistent in what you've been trying to do throughout.

81
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:19,400
Yeah, I think that's pretty accurate.

82
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:20,400
First of all, thank you for having me on.

83
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:23,680
I've listened for a long time, so it's good to finally be on the show.

84
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:28,840
But I do think we do treat it as kind of a job, rather than something where you one day,

85
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,720
some days you wake up and you have kind of the passion for it and you show up to work

86
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:34,640
and some days you don't and you stay in bed.

87
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:38,840
We've always viewed it as a profession and so we try and show up and do the job whether

88
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:40,960
we like it day in or day out.

89
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:45,380
Some days it's worse than others and you don't feel like doing it, but you still put on one

90
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:48,280
boot at a time and show up and do it to a professional degree.

91
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:54,160
We did that through the end of 2022 when things are really bad and into 2023 and then we still

92
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:55,160
do it now.

93
00:05:55,160 --> 00:06:00,160
We haven't changed the way or the pace that we work at just because times are much better

94
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:01,160
now.

95
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:05,120
And a point that I'd highlight is I don't think there are many teams that have been

96
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:10,400
around for that kind of time span, having had a moment where you had to refocus almost

97
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:14,440
or readjust without having completely pivoted into a different category.

98
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:19,560
A lot of these things became restaking protocols one day or went from NFTs to some sort of

99
00:06:19,560 --> 00:06:23,600
modularized lending one day and you guys really stayed true to the credit and capital markets

100
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:24,600
direction.

101
00:06:24,600 --> 00:06:25,600
Yeah.

102
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:26,600
I think we have tried to do that.

103
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,520
Actually, just continuing the thought from the last question, I think two things that

104
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,040
kind of influenced me were I really like this book called What I Talk About When I Talk

105
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,040
About Running by Murakami.

106
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:39,760
He talks about how he gets up, he has a routine when he's writing a book and he gets up every

107
00:06:39,760 --> 00:06:43,840
morning at like four or five in the morning and he writes for five or six hours, then

108
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:48,160
he eats, then he goes for a run in the afternoon and it's just like the craft is getting up

109
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:51,720
and just doing that every day whether he feels like it or not.

110
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:57,000
But on the pivot side, I think we've always thought that to really be successful, you

111
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,440
have to play in where you have an edge.

112
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,480
The closest we ever came to doing something kind of different was early 2023 when there

113
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:08,080
was the craze around tokenized T-bills.

114
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:12,740
We put out a product that was cash management, so it was still structured similar to how

115
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:17,580
we do the over collateralized lending, but the collateral for the loans was T-bills,

116
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:20,600
so the rate you could earn was about the T-bill rate.

117
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,440
What we found though was that it was very competitive.

118
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:27,240
We had very little way of differentiating ourselves and it was a race to the bottom

119
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:28,240
on fees.

120
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:33,240
So we kind of went back to our roots and for us, that is lending and credit and doing

121
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:35,320
that to institutions.

122
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:40,080
And what we observed was that the most contrarian bet you could take at the time in early to

123
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:43,460
mid 2023 was thinking that lending was going to come back.

124
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:45,840
Everybody was ready to totally write off the sector.

125
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:49,360
So we more looked at, okay, if we can't do under collateralized lending, which is what

126
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:52,640
we've done before, is there a form of lending we can do?

127
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:57,440
What is the market saying it will buy at the moment that it's not being sold or not being

128
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:58,720
offered?

129
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:02,200
And clearly it wasn't under collateralized lending at the time.

130
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:06,360
So what we did was we looked at over collateralized lending, where all the main competitors in

131
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:12,060
that segment had blown up or exited the space from the end of 2022.

132
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:14,520
And so we said, okay, we have the skills to do this.

133
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:15,520
Nobody else is doing it.

134
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:19,080
We can price it appropriately and we can get paid for it.

135
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:23,720
And we think over the long term, what are the odds that lending, the oldest service

136
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:26,920
in finance doesn't come back within this space?

137
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:28,000
Probably pretty low.

138
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,240
So that gave us the confidence, I think, to stick with lending as a segment instead of

139
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:40,720
trying to pivot and become a restaker or a tokenization platform or something else.

140
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:45,200
And I have seen a bunch of teams who pivot repeatedly and sometimes it's good to pivot.

141
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:50,200
Slack was a pivot, but sometimes you actually just want to stick it out and work out a different

142
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:53,380
way to kind of hit your same North Star.

143
00:08:53,380 --> 00:08:54,380
It's pretty incredible.

144
00:08:54,380 --> 00:09:00,300
I remember a time in there on 2023 when consensus view in the industry was probably that crypto

145
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:03,720
and CIFI didn't go well together and you had a coin base.

146
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:07,420
But in terms of something like lending, there was an intense skepticism around centralized

147
00:09:07,420 --> 00:09:11,500
lending for crypto, which is nearly ludicrous in retrospect.

148
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:12,500
Yeah.

149
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:16,860
Some of the pain points to the criticisms of the centralized lending at the time, I think

150
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:17,860
were valid.

151
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:23,660
limited transparency and a lot of opaqueness around the performance of the loans.

152
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:25,160
Were they paying or not?

153
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:27,940
Were they collateralized or under collateralized?

154
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,220
What was the equity of the lending company?

155
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:33,580
So how much buffer did they have to absorb losses?

156
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:37,660
Well, those are more fairly valid criticisms, I think.

157
00:09:37,660 --> 00:09:42,380
But it was taken to kind of an absurd degree, which was that lending is dead and doesn't

158
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:46,940
belong on blockchains in the sector and they just do it so much better in traditional finance.

159
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:51,300
If you read enough traditional finance history, you see Citibank blows up every 40 years.

160
00:09:51,300 --> 00:09:54,860
So I don't know that they are doing it so much better than we can.

161
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:59,300
And I think in a new segment, you have to kind of make it up as you go along.

162
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:02,860
And you can take lessons from traditional finance, but you have to adapt it to what

163
00:10:02,860 --> 00:10:04,220
is a nascent market.

164
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:09,100
You can't require every company that you deal with in crypto to have audited financials

165
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:12,080
by a big four accounting firm because that just doesn't exist.

166
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:16,780
So the consensus view was obviously very against lending, but that in turn created the

167
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:18,180
opportunity for us.

168
00:10:18,180 --> 00:10:21,900
If the consensus hadn't been that lending was terrible and there were a bunch of people

169
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:27,140
coming in wanting to offer the same products we do now, then maybe we wouldn't have grown

170
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:29,420
the way that we have over the last 18 months.

171
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:33,220
It's like Howard Marks says, you have to be both non-consensus and correct.

172
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:35,760
If you're consensus and correct, you don't get paid.

173
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:38,220
And if you're non-consensus and wrong, you're toast.

174
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:39,220
You don't make anything.

175
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:40,220
Yeah.

176
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,220
Yeah.

177
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:42,220
You zero it out.

178
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:49,860
How much do you think some of those irresponsible credit activities in 2022, let's say, set

179
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:51,860
us back as an industry?

180
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:58,340
And was that purely a matter of individual actors making rash decisions and some accompanying

181
00:10:58,340 --> 00:10:59,340
contagion?

182
00:10:59,340 --> 00:11:03,620
Or how do you look back on that period and the mass fallout that we had?

183
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:09,620
Well, it definitely set us back, I'd say probably the rest of the duration of the Biden administration.

184
00:11:10,420 --> 00:11:14,760
Certainly at least two years, because it's only now that some of the players who initially

185
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:21,980
had their own blockchain and on-chain project teams doing something, all those teams effectively

186
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:22,980
got shuttered.

187
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:25,500
And I would say it's only now that we've kind of come through to the Trump administration

188
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:30,520
where there is more of an appetite for the industry to be based in the US, that those

189
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:34,240
teams are getting restarted or those people are getting rehired again.

190
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:38,480
So I'd say at least two years, but it's always very difficult to kind of run a Schrodinger's

191
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:42,720
cat type scenario on the probability that didn't play out.

192
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:48,120
But to your other question, I hate it when somebody says the causes are multifactor.

193
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:54,700
I think it was due in a large respect to individual bad actors, but what kind of creates the condition?

194
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:58,920
And in some cases, people who are otherwise good, who just made incorrect decisions at

195
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:04,120
the time, but definitely there were cases of fraud, three arrows, Celsius.

196
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:06,900
But you have to kind of look at the conditions that create that, right?

197
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:09,160
The conditions that created are easy money.

198
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:11,280
Easy money is when more fraud happens.

199
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:15,200
And that is because money was getting pushed into venture and then from venture gas to

200
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:18,040
get pushed into investable businesses.

201
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:21,760
And what happened was that the lending companies got valued based on the size of their loan

202
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:22,760
book.

203
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:27,260
So if you gave out a bad loan, let's say you gave out $100 million of bad loans, you got

204
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:32,800
valued the same as a company that gave out $100 million of good loans, maybe more because

205
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:37,540
it's obviously easier to give out $100 million of bad loans than $100 million of good loans.

206
00:12:37,540 --> 00:12:40,840
It takes longer, more rigorous underwriting.

207
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:43,900
And so those businesses could scale faster.

208
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:49,180
And the game was to get as big as possible so that you could revalue yourself and do

209
00:12:49,180 --> 00:12:51,540
another equity round at a higher valuation.

210
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:58,020
So that bred bad behavior because there was no feedback mechanism to uncover who was swimming

211
00:12:58,020 --> 00:13:02,300
naked until the end of 2022 after we had the lunar blow up and that uncovered a bunch of

212
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:04,040
holes in balance sheets.

213
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:07,760
And to a certain extent, that also drove things like FTX, right?

214
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:14,760
Sam SBF was doing a new round at 20 percent higher valuation every two or three months,

215
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:17,960
right? I remember at the start of 2022, I think he was raising it $10 billion.

216
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:22,000
By the end, it was $30 billion or just before the end of November.

217
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:26,400
So I think the massive amounts of liquidity in the system definitely created distortive

218
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:30,560
behaviors. But this is as Ray Dalio says, this is what you see in every credit bubble.

219
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:32,500
And this is why it's called a credit cycle.

220
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:35,780
And that's just what you have to watch out for the next time.

221
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:39,260
And I think the key in a lending business is that unlike other businesses, you have to

222
00:13:39,260 --> 00:13:45,140
recognize that you never really hit escape velocity from being in a cyclical industry.

223
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:51,180
And so you always have to watch out that at any given cycle, you are still at risk.

224
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:55,820
Your business still faces an existential risk if you don't control your credit and your

225
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:59,900
underwriting and your risk management. And that's kind of unique to credit and lending

226
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:02,400
businesses in all of finance and startups.

227
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:04,240
It's not really the same in other sectors.

228
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:09,200
Yeah, that's it. I do worry about where we'll go to with some of the trends that I'd

229
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:11,520
argue are closer to easy money, like you'd say.

230
00:14:11,520 --> 00:14:15,600
Like, I'm sure we'll get a leveraged Treasury equity vehicle, for example.

231
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:17,880
Yeah, we used to have those in Treadfy, right?

232
00:14:17,960 --> 00:14:20,080
They used to lever up T-bills a lot.

233
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:24,840
I think they still do. But you will have, I think, a Treasury company that probably

234
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:27,560
leverages too much and maybe blows itself up.

235
00:14:28,060 --> 00:14:33,020
But that will be driven by very easy, accommodative monetary conditions.

236
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:38,740
And so we have an advisor to the company and he's been in the lending business for a long

237
00:14:38,740 --> 00:14:42,740
time. And so we catch up with him every few months and we kind of take stock of, on a

238
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:46,060
scale of one to ten, where are we in terms of credit conditions?

239
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:49,620
Ten being the worst and one being the most conservative.

240
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:53,740
And our sense at the moment is that we're still in kind of the first half.

241
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:58,200
It's still more like a four rather than like a seven or an eight or a nine.

242
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:02,120
Whereas mid-2022, you probably would have said that was more like a nine.

243
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:06,920
I remember at that time, rates borrowing against Bitcoin were getting down to like

244
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:12,960
two, three percent. Unsecured rates lending Bitcoin were 10 to 20 basis points.

245
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:16,480
And the dollar unsecured rates were six percent.

246
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:19,200
So fully unsecured lending.

247
00:15:19,380 --> 00:15:24,620
Whereas here today, rates lending against Bitcoin overcollateralized are probably in

248
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:26,300
the seven to eight range.

249
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:29,540
So I'd say we're still in a much more conservative set of conditions.

250
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:33,660
And I don't see that kind of frothy euphoria that we did in 2022 yet.

251
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:39,740
Yeah, I tend to agree. Talking about some of these systems, how did you decide to go

252
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:41,700
and try and build DeFi credit markets?

253
00:15:41,780 --> 00:15:44,420
Or what inspired you to move that direction, given your experience?

254
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:47,780
So my experience was I used to work in TranFi banking.

255
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:51,680
So I used to be in an area called securitization and debt capital markets.

256
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:54,960
This meant basically we issued bonds and we helped companies to issue bonds.

257
00:15:54,960 --> 00:15:58,800
And that was how they borrowed. So they borrowed large amounts like, let's say,

258
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:02,040
five hundred million. And then they break that into small chunks and they make

259
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:03,520
five hundred thousand dollar loans.

260
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:08,360
So I always kind of had lending in the DNA, but I wanted to go to a more

261
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:09,920
entrepreneurial company from there.

262
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:15,040
So I went to a smaller commercial finance company that still exists in Australia

263
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:17,220
today. That was where I met my co-founder.

264
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:22,500
And the initial idea was to do tokenized bonds on chain.

265
00:16:22,840 --> 00:16:23,980
So that was really tough.

266
00:16:24,020 --> 00:16:28,700
There was no DeFi at the time that we started the company was kind of five or six

267
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:29,500
teams.

268
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:32,780
Was that for distribution or what inspired that?

269
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:40,940
The idea was that if you could lower the cost of issuing bonds, you could make it

270
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:44,900
possible for the longer tail of finance companies to come in and issue them.

271
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:48,920
Because at the time, it was more of the case that it was almost not worth

272
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:51,840
issuing securitized bonds if you couldn't do a hundred million at a time.

273
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:57,280
So you can see most companies were taking five years of operations to get there.

274
00:16:57,280 --> 00:16:59,800
So you could just bring that forward and help those companies.

275
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:03,400
What we found, though, was there was nothing to tokenize on chain.

276
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:08,960
So all of DeFi was Maker, Set Protocol, Compound, Uniswap.

277
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:13,840
I don't think Aave had not rebranded from E-Flint at that point in time.

278
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:16,300
So really you had a very small on-chain ecosystem.

279
00:17:16,820 --> 00:17:20,540
And so what we found was we instead had to go more of a direct lending route and look

280
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:23,020
to just lend to institutions who would borrow.

281
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:27,660
But the initial way that we wanted to do it was back in 2018, 2019, everybody was

282
00:17:27,660 --> 00:17:28,980
obsessed with marketplaces.

283
00:17:28,980 --> 00:17:31,380
You had to be an asset-like marketplace.

284
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:35,220
God forbid you're asset heavy because you're not going to make the same returns on

285
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:39,460
equity. As we tried doing that, and that was the initial iteration of Maple, was

286
00:17:39,460 --> 00:17:43,220
you'd have other credit managers called delegates come on and they would earn a

287
00:17:43,220 --> 00:17:47,680
FB, like an independent contractor, and we would earn a platform fee because

288
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:51,900
everybody wanted to be like Stripe or Uber or Airbnb, and you clip your platform

289
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:56,720
fee and other people take a portion of the revenue and then you're kind of neutral.

290
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:59,480
So you can scale much easier because you don't have to hire staff.

291
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,880
You just allow other people to sign up and use your infrastructure.

292
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:05,560
And that was kind of working out to begin with.

293
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:07,640
So we got the delegates up and running.

294
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:12,320
We thought that we would not have any reputational impacts if delegates had credit

295
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:14,020
issues with the loans they underwrote.

296
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:19,580
And we thought we would be able to scale much faster being a kind of asset-like

297
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:23,340
marketplace. And you get the asset-like marketplace multiples, right, in terms of

298
00:18:23,340 --> 00:18:24,660
how people rate your company.

299
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:28,340
I think what you can see, though, in the time since then is that actually there's a

300
00:18:28,340 --> 00:18:30,700
lot of value in being asset heavy.

301
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:32,100
Asset heavy is kind of a moat.

302
00:18:32,260 --> 00:18:34,620
Vertical integration gives you pricing power.

303
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:40,180
To be vertically integrated, you just have to have more plant and equipment and

304
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:44,760
capital. And so that means you're asset heavy, but you get the pricing power and

305
00:18:44,760 --> 00:18:46,360
the pricing power gives you longevity.

306
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:47,800
And you can see it in Apple.

307
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:52,520
Apple didn't say we want to be an asset light piece of hardware that supports any

308
00:18:52,520 --> 00:18:55,240
kind of software, nor did they want to say we just want to have the app store and

309
00:18:55,240 --> 00:18:57,760
it's on available on any hardware device.

310
00:18:57,800 --> 00:18:59,400
They said, we've got to have the both of them.

311
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:03,080
And you can see that for them, they make all of the profits in the smartphone

312
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:05,960
sector, right, despite being a minority of the market.

313
00:19:06,880 --> 00:19:08,320
And I think you can see it with Tesla as well.

314
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:12,460
They make their software, they make their hardware and across history, economic

315
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:15,180
history as well. You can see numerous examples of this going right back to

316
00:19:15,180 --> 00:19:20,540
Standard Oil. And so I think that is how our model shifted over 2023.

317
00:19:20,540 --> 00:19:22,940
We said, OK, we're going to remove the delegates.

318
00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:27,580
We will be the sole kind of underwriter here and we will therefore control who

319
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:32,140
gets to borrow and we will actually be the borrower ourselves.

320
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:35,420
So we borrow from people who put capital on the platform and then we lend it out

321
00:19:35,420 --> 00:19:36,860
and we keep a spread.

322
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:39,120
But it allowed us to move faster.

323
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:42,880
We doubled our revenue because we weren't paying our fees to third parties on the

324
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:46,920
platform. And I think over time, you can see that that's kind of proved

325
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:49,080
strategically the correct way to go.

326
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:53,800
Notwithstanding that a number of our venture backers at the time said this is

327
00:19:53,800 --> 00:19:57,120
going to prevent you from scaling as fast as you could if you were just

328
00:19:57,120 --> 00:20:02,520
infrastructure. And they have pointed to other lending markets that have grown

329
00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:05,120
quite substantially over that period of time.

330
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:08,900
But I would say that there are some of those lending markets who make a lot of

331
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:10,300
revenue and are doing very well.

332
00:20:10,300 --> 00:20:14,180
There are some who actually make no revenue despite being larger in terms of

333
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:18,900
TVL than us. And that's because other parties, they have weak negotiating

334
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:21,620
positions and other parties have effectively taken the majority of the

335
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:23,860
revenue there. So we have a better negotiating position.

336
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:27,980
And so I think vertical integration rather than just being a pure marketplace

337
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:30,340
is actually in the end, the better way to go.

338
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:32,300
Yeah, I think it's a great point.

339
00:20:32,400 --> 00:20:36,880
There was such a push towards being asset light because I look at it as an

340
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:42,880
existential problem in DeFi that everyone sees themselves as just capital

341
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:47,920
aggregation layers or a capital marketplace without any actual tie to the

342
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:50,320
capital. But that makes it naturally very fleeting.

343
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:53,920
And then you had this idea of protocol owned liquidity, which I think made a lot

344
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:56,920
of sense in that vein of like, OK, you actually have some control of it for the

345
00:20:56,920 --> 00:21:01,840
first time. Yeah, I think protocol owned liquidity gave teams a better balance

346
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:06,580
sheet and wasn't necessarily executed well by some of the teams that did it,

347
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:08,980
like Olympus. But the concept, I think, was sound.

348
00:21:08,980 --> 00:21:13,140
But getting a balance sheet gives you heft and gives you negotiating power.

349
00:21:13,580 --> 00:21:18,500
In the Web2 world, you can look at eBay, which continued to practice being asset

350
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:21,300
light. And then you can look at Amazon that built its own fulfillment centers.

351
00:21:21,300 --> 00:21:25,620
And ultimately, the fulfillment centers allowed them to vertically integrate.

352
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:28,540
They could sell the good and then they could drop it to your house.

353
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,920
Whereas eBay relied on third parties for that.

354
00:21:31,920 --> 00:21:36,040
And so the pricing power eventually finds its way to the third parties.

355
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:39,560
My view is that it's actually very important for teams to kind of vertically

356
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:41,800
integrate so that they get pricing power.

357
00:21:41,800 --> 00:21:43,720
They have a balance sheet they can swing around.

358
00:21:44,120 --> 00:21:48,800
And nowadays, we also get pitched a lot of deals where we can get extra spoken

359
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:54,000
incentives and things like that if we're lending to a certain company or

360
00:21:54,020 --> 00:21:58,820
counterparty or partnering with them in some way, just because we now sort of

361
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:01,740
have that heft in terms of AUM in the pools.

362
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:07,060
So to summarize the conversation with regard to Maple, Maple was started as an

363
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:08,620
on-chain credit marketplace.

364
00:22:08,940 --> 00:22:12,500
Is that how you would describe it today or how has the platform evolved and how

365
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:13,740
have you intentionally involved it?

366
00:22:14,180 --> 00:22:16,660
So we would have started the marketplace.

367
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:21,020
I think the first thing we dropped was we became more of a lender and we started

368
00:22:21,020 --> 00:22:24,040
marketing ourselves as a direct lender rather than a marketplace.

369
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:28,960
And the latest reimagining of what we are, reimagining is probably not the

370
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:32,120
right word, but the most apt description of what we are today is more of an

371
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:32,840
asset manager.

372
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:36,120
So we call ourselves an on-chain asset manager.

373
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:40,360
Our primary focus is lending and credit and yield.

374
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:42,720
And we run a couple of strategies.

375
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:46,440
The first and the largest is the over-collateralized lending.

376
00:22:46,440 --> 00:22:48,920
So we are a secured lender to institutions.

377
00:22:49,340 --> 00:22:53,940
We have a loan book of over a billion dollars and typically our loan sizes will

378
00:22:53,940 --> 00:22:57,620
be anywhere from five mill at the low end to a hundred mill plus.

379
00:22:57,660 --> 00:22:59,580
You know, we actually just did a hundred mill one today.

380
00:23:00,180 --> 00:23:02,580
And then the other line is we have a BTC yield product.

381
00:23:02,580 --> 00:23:07,460
So it's encompassed within the term asset manager, on-chain asset manager.

382
00:23:07,820 --> 00:23:12,100
And if you look the TradFi peers or competitors, I would point to would be

383
00:23:12,100 --> 00:23:14,180
Apollo, Aries, Blackstone.

384
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:18,920
These guys are the largest non-bank lenders in the world, but they don't

385
00:23:18,920 --> 00:23:20,880
call themselves lenders.

386
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:22,640
What they call themselves is asset managers.

387
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:24,680
And I think we think about ourselves in the same vein.

388
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:28,720
We're an asset manager with a focus on credit and lending, and ultimately we

389
00:23:28,720 --> 00:23:30,320
want to be competing with them.

390
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:34,280
Our view is that all lending over the next 10 years is going to be done in

391
00:23:34,280 --> 00:23:38,760
stable coins, and so we are getting a leg up in kind of surfing that wave.

392
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:42,560
And that will bring with it cost advantages, distribution advantages,

393
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:46,140
speed advantages that will allow us to take share.

394
00:23:46,740 --> 00:23:49,860
How do you create stickiness in the assets that you guys manage?

395
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:51,440
There's a couple of ways you can go.

396
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:55,020
So you can either do pricing or quality, I think.

397
00:23:55,100 --> 00:24:00,860
And in lending and asset management, pricing is effectively, do you minimize

398
00:24:00,860 --> 00:24:04,300
your own spread or deliver a higher yield to your customers?

399
00:24:04,740 --> 00:24:07,660
But the other thing you can do is you can offer a lower volatility.

400
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:09,620
So more stability in that pricing.

401
00:24:09,640 --> 00:24:14,920
And so I would say versus DeFi peers, we tend to produce a higher yield or return.

402
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:19,080
And a couple of the reasons for that is that we serve institutions who like

403
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:24,880
hosting BTC and native assets instead of wrapping those assets for a variety of

404
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:29,480
tax compliance and smart contract risk reasons, but we can offer that.

405
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:33,520
And your average DeFi protocol can't accept native Bitcoin.

406
00:24:33,880 --> 00:24:35,960
And so the customers pay a little bit more for that.

407
00:24:35,980 --> 00:24:39,900
We can also offer a qualified custody because we are more integrated

408
00:24:39,900 --> 00:24:46,220
now with CFI providers like Anchorage, Bitgo, Coinbase, Custody, Zodiac

409
00:24:46,220 --> 00:24:51,420
Custody, Fireblocks, and a number of other wallet and custody solutions.

410
00:24:51,420 --> 00:24:52,260
Copper as well.

411
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:57,060
So that optionality, you also pay a premium for if you're an institutional client.

412
00:24:57,060 --> 00:25:01,180
And so generally we deliver a higher yield than the DeFi lending protocols.

413
00:25:01,660 --> 00:25:05,140
And so that's kind of pricing and quality, but we also tend to be

414
00:25:05,140 --> 00:25:09,000
more stable over time because we don't have utilization-based rates.

415
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:12,880
We can offer fixed rates and again, institutions like to be able to lock

416
00:25:12,880 --> 00:25:15,120
in certainty on their borrowing costs.

417
00:25:15,480 --> 00:25:17,640
And so our rates don't fluctuate over time.

418
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:22,440
You'll never see a day where the maple rate goes from 6% to 9% and then the

419
00:25:22,440 --> 00:25:26,640
following day is back at 4%, like you do on some of the DeFi protocols.

420
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:28,440
Many of them, I would add.

421
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:33,080
Yeah, it will be a steadier 6.5% to 7% over two months.

422
00:25:33,540 --> 00:25:36,620
I've done some research on Apollo and some of these large credit firms.

423
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:41,980
And I would say roughly, let's say, cause they're in private credit, some 30, 40%

424
00:25:41,980 --> 00:25:45,300
of what they allocate to is investment grade credit opportunities.

425
00:25:45,300 --> 00:25:51,220
How would you compare where Maple is allocating as an asset manager to that set?

426
00:25:51,620 --> 00:25:57,740
They are very fundamentally like we all fall within the ages of institutional

427
00:25:58,180 --> 00:25:59,580
credit or private credit.

428
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:04,880
However, they have a $300 billion plus portfolio, so they're naturally going

429
00:26:04,880 --> 00:26:06,640
to be doing a wider variety of things.

430
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:08,520
And they've built their business up over 40 years.

431
00:26:08,760 --> 00:26:13,560
Apollo was started in the late eighties after Drexel Burnham by some

432
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:14,840
of the alumni from there.

433
00:26:15,360 --> 00:26:18,720
And so they've had 40 years to build a very well diversified business.

434
00:26:19,260 --> 00:26:24,460
And I would say our credit, we fall within the unrated category because the

435
00:26:24,460 --> 00:26:29,280
company we lend to our primes, OTC desks, funds and exchanges, but we do

436
00:26:29,300 --> 00:26:32,700
take what I consider to be very good quality collateral.

437
00:26:32,700 --> 00:26:37,220
We're taking Bitcoin at trades over 50 billion a day, including Sundays.

438
00:26:37,220 --> 00:26:41,380
And so we always have very good liquidity and downside protection when it comes to

439
00:26:41,380 --> 00:26:44,820
that, but yeah, so I'd say it's institutional grade quality.

440
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:49,660
It's not rated yet by ratings agencies, but I do think that is going to be a

441
00:26:49,660 --> 00:26:52,180
step that comes in in the next one to two years.

442
00:26:52,500 --> 00:26:54,720
So my background was securitization.

443
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:56,780
I would love to start securitizing this stuff.

444
00:26:56,800 --> 00:27:00,640
And I think that is ultimately how we're going to go and package Bitcoin backed

445
00:27:00,640 --> 00:27:06,480
loans up and sell them to the next wave of investors who will come in, which

446
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:11,640
will be TradFi firms that don't necessarily want to buy an allocation to

447
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:13,720
loans done through a smart contract.

448
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:19,080
But what they might want to buy is a senior trench in a rated securitization

449
00:27:19,080 --> 00:27:22,720
vehicle that has 200 Bitcoin backed loans sitting underneath it.

450
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:25,920
And that's ultimately where I think the puck is going over time.

451
00:27:26,340 --> 00:27:32,180
And it brings in more capital, a wider set of investors, and ultimately

452
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:33,900
allows a firm like us to scale.

453
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:39,020
Securitizations is a major way that firms like Apollo and Aries and

454
00:27:39,020 --> 00:27:40,580
Blackstone source their capital.

455
00:27:40,580 --> 00:27:43,980
It's not just directly raising money into fund structures.

